.ss 12 0
.SH NAME
cgpaint \- a paint program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cgpaint
.RI [ "toolkit options" ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cgpaint
draws pictures, as the Paint of Windows 98 did. Its window has the
picture in the middle, a box of sixteen tools at the left, a box of
colours at the bottom, and beneath that a line that says what the
chosen tool is for, which point of the picture the pointer is at, and
how large the thing being drawn is.
.PP
The left button draws in the foreground's colour and the right button
in the background's. Each takes its colour from the colour box in the
same way, and the two are shown at the box's left, the foreground's
over the background's. Pressing a colour of the box twice changes it,
as
.B Colors \(-> Edit Colors
does for the one last taken.
.PP
The picture starts white, 640 points by 480. The three handles at its
right, at its bottom and at their corner give it another size when
dragged, and so does
.BR "Image \(-> Attributes" .
What is added is of the background's colour.
.SH TOOLS
The box beneath the tools holds what the chosen tool can be told, and
the choice made there is marked.
.TP
.B Free-Form Select, Select
Drag round a part of the picture, or a rectangle over it. What is
selected can then be dragged elsewhere, leaving the background's
colour where it was; with Control held, it leaves a copy of itself.
It is laid down when something else is done: a press outside it,
another tool, Escape. The box chooses whether it is laid down whole or
without what in it is of the background's colour, as
.B Image \(-> Draw Opaque
also does.
.TP
.B Eraser
Rubs out with the background's colour, in one of four sizes. The right
button rubs out only what is of the foreground's colour.
.TP
.B Fill With Color
Fills the area of one colour that the pointer is in.
.TP
.B Pick Color
Takes the colour under the pointer, for the foreground with the left
button and for the background with the right; then the tool that was
chosen before is chosen again.
.TP
.B Magnifier
Shows the picture four times its size, about the point pressed, and at
its own size again when pressed there; the box has one, two, six and
eight times.
.TP
.B Pencil
Draws a line one point wide.
.TP
.B Brush
Draws with one of twelve brushes: round, square, and slanted either
way, in three sizes.
.TP
.B Airbrush
Sprays points for as long as the button is down, in one of three
sizes.
.TP
.B Text
Drag out a box and type. The text breaks at the box's edge, and the
box grows downwards as it has to; BackSpace takes the last character
away. It is laid down as a selection is, and can be dragged while it
is typed. The font is chosen in
.BR "View \(-> Text Font" ,
and the box chooses whether the text has the background's colour
behind it.
.TP
.B Line
Draws a straight line, one to five points wide.
.TP
.B Curve
Draw a straight line; then drag anywhere to bend it, and once more to
bend it again.
.TP
.B Rectangle, Ellipse, Rounded Rectangle
Drag from corner to corner. The box chooses the edge alone, the edge
with the other button's colour inside, or the whole in one colour. The
edge is as wide as the Line tool's.
.TP
.B Polygon
Drag the first side, then press where each next corner is to be.
Pressing twice, or near the first corner, closes it.
.PP
With Shift held, a line keeps level, upright or at half a right angle,
and a shape is as wide as it is high. The other button, pressed while
one is down, undoes what that one was doing, and so does Escape.
.SH MENUS
.TP
.B File
New, Open, Save and Save As. A picture that has changed is asked about
before it goes.
.TP
.B Edit
Undo and Repeat; Cut, Copy and Paste, which use the clipboard, where a
picture is a PNG file; Clear Selection and Select All;
.BR "Copy To" ,
which saves what is selected as a file, and
.BR "Paste From" ,
which pastes a file. What is pasted is selected, at the top left of
what the window shows.
.TP
.B View
Shows and hides the tool box, the colour box and the status bar.
.B Zoom
has the picture's own size, four times that, any of one, two, four,
six and eight times, and a grid between the points, which is drawn
from four times up.
.TP
.B Image
.B Flip and Rotate
turns the picture over or by quarters of a circle;
.B Stretch and Skew
makes it wider or higher by hundredths, and slants it by degrees;
.B Invert Colors
gives every point its opposite. All three work on what is selected, if
anything is, and otherwise on the whole picture.
.B Clear Image
makes it all the background's colour.
.SH FILES
Three kinds of file are read and written: Windows bitmaps, XPM and
PNG. A file says itself what it is when it is read. When one is
written, the end of its name says:
.BR .bmp ,
.B .xpm
or
.BR .png ;
a name with none of these is given
.BR .bmp .
.PP
A bitmap is written with 24 bits to a point. Bitmaps of 1, 4, 8, 24
and 32 bits are read, with a header of Windows or of OS/2, unless they
are packed. A picture has nothing that is seen through: where a PNG
file or an XPM file has, the picture is white.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Say how it is used, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Say which version it is, and exit.
.PP
The usual toolkit options, such as
.BR \-\-display ,
are understood too.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Control+N, Control+O, Control+S, Control+Q
New, Open, Save, Exit.
.TP
.B Control+Z, Control+Y
Undo, Repeat.
.TP
.B Control+X, Control+C, Control+V, Delete, Control+A
Cut, Copy, Paste, Clear Selection, Select All.
.TP
.B Control+T, Control+L
Show or hide the tool box, the colour box.
.TP
.B Control+Page Up, Control+Page Down, Control+G
The picture's own size, four times that, the grid.
.TP
.B Control+R, Control+W, Control+I, Control+E, Control+Shift+N
Flip and Rotate, Stretch and Skew, Invert Colors, Attributes, Clear
Image.
.SH BUGS
What is selected cannot be stretched by handles, text is typed at its
end only, and nothing is printed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cghex (1),
.BR cgcalc (1),
.BR cgrun (1)
